# NetQoS — Adaptive Packet Scheduling and Quality of Service Management

This document outlines the architecture, design, and phased development plan for building the NetQoS system. The core of this project is demonstrating adaptive packet queue scheduling using actual Python sockets to generate and manage network traffic.

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> Please review the proposed architecture and phased execution plan. 
> Since this involves raw socket programming to measure latency and simulate queues, we will use an application-level simulation of network queues and scheduling. That is, the sender will put generated packets into our application-level queues, and our scheduler will pull from these queues to send them over actual loopback/local sockets to the receiver. Is this application-level queuing approach acceptable?

## Open Questions

> [!WARNING]
> 1. **Networking Interface:** Do you want the traffic to be routed strictly locally (e.g., `127.0.0.1`) for demonstration and testing to avoid any firewall or security issues?
> 2. **Packet Structure:** To measure latency and jitter accurately, do you agree to injecting custom application-layer headers into the TCP/UDP payloads (e.g., `[timestamp|flow_id|seq_num|payload]`)? This is necessary because raw socket access requires root/admin privileges, which we are avoiding per the safety requirements.

